enced></math>   <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>ii</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfrac></math>   <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>iii</mi><mo>)</mo></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>·</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>It is given</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F5b6eb6e7-1086-4057-8b85-750395666a01-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F5b6eb6e7-1086-4057-8b85-750395666a01-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F5b6eb6e7-1086-4057-8b85-750395666a01-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 210px; height: 123px;\" /></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi>pF</mi></math></p><p>Using given values</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo> </mo><mi>pF</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Hard","source":"BITSAT 2017","_marksId":"64c0e7e86f5bfc7ab78ae8e8"},{"id":18820,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"In the figure below, what is the potential difference between the point $A$ and $B$ and between $B$ and $C$ respectively in steady state\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F05n1XZBNDOJvFz3awJy97ZmZHCfchGzAyeAunMvSmJM.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F05n1XZBNDOJvFz3awJy97ZmZHCfchGzAyeAunMvSmJM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F05n1XZBNDOJvFz3awJy97ZmZHCfchGzAyeAunMvSmJM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\quad V_{A B}=V_{B C}=100 \\mathrm{~V}$","$\\quad V_{A B}=75 V, V_{B C}=25 V$","$\\quad V_{A B}=25 V, V_{B C}=75 V$","$\\quad V_{A B}=V_{B C}=50 \\mathrm{~V}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"The equivalent circuit is shown in figure.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\begin{array}{l}\r\n\r\nV_{1}+V_{2}=100 \\\\\r\n\r\n\\text { and } 2 V_{1}=6 V_{2}\r\n\r\n\\end{array}\r\n\r\n$<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252Fs2O76azAVpnq35rd4-HqwdGjim3m6EWgcmopPQHMzZQ.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fs2O76azAVpnq35rd4-HqwdGjim3m6EWgcmopPQHMzZQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2Fs2O76azAVpnq35rd4-HqwdGjim3m6EWgcmopPQHMzZQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">$ \\\\ \r\n\r\nOn solving above equations, we get $V_{1}=75 V, V_{2}=25 V$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2016","_marksId":"64c0e7d76f5bfc7ab78ae850"},{"id":18821,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"A parallel plate capacitor of area ' $A$ ' plate separation ' $d$ ' is filled with two dielectrics as shown. What is the capacitance of the arrangement?\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FUwdipp_XL3YUGOKXcHsavEPIHKMk-pr-KF77AodoFoU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FUwdipp_XL3YUGOKXcHsavEPIHKMk-pr-KF77AodoFoU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FUwdipp_XL3YUGOKXcHsavEPIHKMk-pr-KF77AodoFoU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\frac{3 K \\varepsilon_{0} A}{4 d}$","$\\frac{4 K \\varepsilon_{0} A}{3 d}$","$\\frac{(K+1) \\varepsilon_{0} A}{2 d}$","$\\frac{K(K+3) \\varepsilon_{0} A}{2(K+1) d}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"\r\n\r\nc_{1}=\\frac{(A / 2) \\varepsilon_{0}}{d / 2}=\\frac{A \\varepsilon_{0}}{d}, c_{2}=K \\frac{A \\varepsilon_{0}}{d}, c_{3}=K \\frac{A \\varepsilon_{0}}{2 d}\r\n\r\n$<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252F-2J2c5Qbdiu4RPjNxqVYEm9IxIFFFQQ25PfTCZIJjOo.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F-2J2c5Qbdiu4RPjNxqVYEm9IxIFFFQQ25PfTCZIJjOo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2F-2J2c5Qbdiu4RPjNxqVYEm9IxIFFFQQ25PfTCZIJjOo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">$ \\\\ \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\therefore \\quad c_{e q .}=\\frac{c_{1} \\times c_{2}}{c_{1}+c_{2}}+c_{3}=\\frac{(3+\\mathrm{K}) \\mathrm{K} A \\varepsilon_{0}}{2 d(\\mathrm{~K}+1)}\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n$\\left(\\because C_{1}\\right.$ and $C_{2}$ are in series and resultant of these two in parallel with $C_{3}$ )","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2016","_marksId":"64c0e7d86f5bfc7ab78ae85c"},{"id":18822,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"A parallel plate capacitor of capacitance $\\mathrm{C}$ is connected to a battery and is charged to a potential difference $\\mathrm{V}$. Another capacitor of capacitance $2 \\mathrm{C}$ is similarly charge to a potential difference $2 \\mathrm{~V}$. The charging battery is now disconnected and the capacitors are connected in parallel to each other in such a way that the positive terminal of one is connected to the negative terminal of the other. The final energy of the configuration is","options":["Zero","$\\frac{3}{2} \\mathrm{CV}^{2}$","$\\frac{25}{6} \\mathrm{CV}^{2}$","$\\frac{9}{2} \\mathrm{CV}^{2}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"From the figure. The net charge shared between the capacitors\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nQ^{\\prime}=Q_{2}-Q_{2}=4 C V-C V=3 C V\r\n\r\n$<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FuC14fhROzIYYJ0pa2wGnML5VYknjH1G83FGfD57wcRo.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FuC14fhROzIYYJ0pa2wGnML5VYknjH1G83FGfD57wcRo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FuC14fhROzIYYJ0pa2wGnML5VYknjH1G83FGfD57wcRo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">$ \\\\ \r\n\r\nThe two capacitors will have some potential, say $\\mathrm{V}^{\\prime}$. The net capacitance of the parallel combination of the two capacitors\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nC^{\\prime}=C_{1}+C_{2}=C+2 C+3 C\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe potential of the capacitors\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\mathrm{V}^{\\prime}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}^{\\prime}}{\\mathrm{C}^{\\prime}}=\\frac{3 \\mathrm{CV}}{3 \\mathrm{C}}=\\mathrm{V}\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe electrostatic energy of the capacitors\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\mathrm{E}^{\\prime}=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{C}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{V}^{\\prime 2}=\\frac{1}{2}(3 \\mathrm{C}) \\mathrm{V}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\mathrm{CV}^{2}\r\n\r\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2015","_marksId":"64c0e7cb6f5bfc7ab78ae7ee"},{"id":18823,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Three capacitors $\\mathrm{C}_{1}=1 \\mu \\mathrm{F}, \\mathrm{C}_{2}=2 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{3}=3\\mu \\mathrm{F}$ are connected as shown in figure, then the equivalent capacitance between points A and $\\mathrm{B}$ is\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FnKt5fLXSPwjtsXbOg4wO8EatBqST2InQ8Z6UkJFzx0U.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FnKt5fLXSPwjtsXbOg4wO8EatBqST2InQ8Z6UkJFzx0U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FnKt5fLXSPwjtsXbOg4wO8EatBqST2InQ8Z6UkJFzx0U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$3 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$","$4 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$","$5 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$","$6 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$C_{\\mathrm{eq}}=(1+2+3) \\mu \\mathrm{F}=6 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$\r\n\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FT3_BJa5_QUYp1OR9JBd76KOMdM-IxehcKrjQY5bDF_U.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FT3_BJa5_QUYp1OR9JBd76KOMdM-IxehcKrjQY5bDF_U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FT3_BJa5_QUYp1OR9JBd76KOMdM-IxehcKrjQY5bDF_U.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2014","_marksId":"64c0e7bb6f5bfc7ab78ae763"},{"id":18824,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Two identical capacitors, have the same capacitance $\\mathrm{C}$. One of them is charged to potential $\\mathrm{V}_{1}$ and the other to $\\mathrm{V}_{2}$. The negative ends of the capacitors are connected together. When the positive ends are also connected, the decrease in energy of the combined system is -","options":["$\\frac{1}{4} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{1}^{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{2}^{2}\\right)$","$\\frac{1}{4} C\\left(V_{1}^{2}+V_{2}^{2}\\right)$","$\\frac{1}{4} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{1}-\\mathrm{V}_{2}\\right)^{2}$","$\\frac{1}{4} C\\left(V_{1}+V_{2}\\right)^{2}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Initial energy of combined system\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\mathrm{U}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{CV}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{CV}_{2}^{2}\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFinal common potential, $\\mathrm{V}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}_{1}+\\mathrm{V}_{2}}{2},$\r\n\r\nFinal energy of system,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\\mathrm{U}_{2}=2 \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{V}_{1}+\\mathrm{V}_{2}}{2}\\right)^{2}\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nHence loss of energy $=\\mathrm{U}_{1}-\\mathrm{U}_{2}$\r\n\r\n=\\frac{1}{4} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{1}-\\mathrm{V}_{2}\\right)^{2}\r\n\r\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2013","_marksId":"64c0e7ad6f5bfc7ab78ae6eb"},{"id":18825,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Two capacitors $\\mathrm{C}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{2}$ are charged to $120 \\mathrm{~V}$ and $200 \\mathrm{~V}$ respectively. It is found that by connecting them together the potential on each one can be made zero. Then","options":["$5 C_{1}=3 C_{2}$","$3 C_{1}=5 C_{2}$","$3 C_{1}+5 C_{2}=0$","$9 C_{1}=4 C_{2}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fbitsat%252FjJt7_1JooJMVJ7r7l-Vi3gZJm26QwjPZ7jK8J2r7ps8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FjJt7_1JooJMVJ7r7l-Vi3gZJm26QwjPZ7jK8J2r7ps8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fbitsat%2FjJt7_1JooJMVJ7r7l-Vi3gZJm26QwjPZ7jK8J2r7ps8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\r\nFor potential to be made zero, after connection\r\n$120 \\mathrm{C}_{1}=200 \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\quad\\left[\\because C=\\frac{q}{v}\\right]\\Rightarrow 3 C_{1}=5 C_{2}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"BITSAT 2012","_marksId":"64c0e79e6f5bfc7ab78ae66c"},{"id":18826,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"If the potential of a capacitor having capacity $6 \\mu \\mathrm{F}$ is increased from $10 \\mathrm{~V}$ to $20 \\mathrm{~V}$, then increase in its energywill be","options":["$4 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~J}$","$4 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~J}$","$9 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~J}$","$12 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~J}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"Capacitance of capacitor (C) $=6 \\mu \\mathrm{F}=6 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~F}$; Initial potential $\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right)=10 \\mathrm{~V}$ and\nfinal potential $\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}\\right)=20 \\mathrm{~V}$.\nThe increase in energy $(\\Delta \\mathrm{U})$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n=\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}^{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\\n=\\frac{1}{2} \\times\\left(6 \\times 10^{-6}\\right) \\times\\left[(20)^{2}-(10)^{2}\\right] \\\\\n=\\left(3 \\times 10^{-6}\\right) \\times 300=9 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~J} .\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2011","_marksId":"64c0e78f6f5bfc7ab78ae5ee"},{"id":18827,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Capacitance","exam":"Engineering","examName":"BITSAT","q":"Calculate the area of the plates of a one farad parallel plate capacitor if separation between plates is $1 \\mathrm{~mm}$ and plates are in vacuum","options":["$18 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m}^{2}$","$0.3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m}^{2}$","$1.3 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m}^{2}$","$1.13 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m}^{2}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"For a parallel plate capacitor $\\mathrm{C}=\\frac{\\varepsilon_{0} \\mathrm{~A}}{\\mathrm{~d}}$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\therefore \\quad & \\mathrm{A}=\\frac{\\mathrm{Cd}}{\\varepsilon_{0}}=\\frac{1 \\times 10^{-3}}{8.85 \\times 10^{-12}} \\\\\n&=1.13 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{~m}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\nThis corresponds to area of square of side $10.6 \\mathrm{~km}$ which shows that one farad is very large unit of capacitance.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"BITSAT 2009","_marksId":"64c0e7736f5bfc7ab78ae4f1"}]}